<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="page6-continuous-part2" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Continuous Games and Stackelberg's Duopoly, Part 2</title>

  <p>
    We're going to continue with the same example from the previous page, but
    we'll make the model more realistic by refusing to let production and
    price be negative. This is covered in Subsection 1.10.2, and it means
    we'll have to consider boundary cases when looking for a maximum.
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-1-10-2">
    <title>Subsection 1.10.2, The Second Model</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read the rest of Section 1.10 (that is, Subsection 1.10.2).</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      The first model let production levels and prices go negative, which
      isn't something that happens in an actual market. This subsection fixes
      that. As you read, keep track of how the fix makes things more
      complicated mathematically.
    </p>
    <p>
      There's a lot in common with Part 1, but we have to address some nuanced
      points now that the model carries further restrictions. You can move
      through those quickly on a first reading; we'll go over them more
      carefully in the notes below.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    The general procedure is the same as in Part 1, but some of the reasoning
    is hard to follow in the book, so we'll break it down further here. Start
    with the two piecewise profit functions. Here is Firm 1's, labeled:
  </p>

  <me>
    \pi_1(s,t) =
    \begin{cases}
      \overbrace{\bigl[20 - 2(s+t)\bigr]s - 4s}^{\substack{\text{exactly the payoff from the first model:} \\ \text{price is still } 20 - 2(s+t) \text{ in this range}}}
        \amp \text{if } 0 \leq s+t \lt 10, \\[4ex]
      \underbrace{-4s}_{\substack{\text{price has bottomed out at } 0, \text{ so revenue is } 0 \cdot s = 0; \\ \text{profit is } 0 - 4s, \text{ a pure loss of the production cost}}}
        \amp \text{if } s+t \geq 10.
    \end{cases}
  </me>

  <p>
    Firm 2's is the mirror image, with <m>t</m> in the role of <m>s</m>:
  </p>

  <me>
    \pi_2(s,t) =
    \begin{cases}
      \bigl[20 - 2(s+t)\bigr]t - 4t \amp \text{if } 0 \leq s+t \lt 10, \\[1ex]
      -4t \amp \text{if } s+t \geq 10.
    \end{cases}
  </me>

  <p>
    The book brushes over some of the reasoning, so let's take a closer look
    at Firm 2's payoff function. Drag the slider below. The shaded region
    marks the production levels that actually earn Firm 2 a profit. Push
    <m>s</m> upward and watch what happens to it.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-firm2-profitable-window">
    <caption>
      Firm 2's profit in the second model, for a value of <m>s</m> you
      control. The shaded region is where profit is positive; the marker is
      Firm 2's best response.
    </caption>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-firm2-profitable-window"
      iframe="ch1-graph/firm2-profitable-window.html" width="100%" aspect="10:9">
      <static>
        <p>
          A slider sets Firm 1's production level s from 0 to 10. A plot of
          Firm 2's profit against its own production t shows a downward
          parabola that becomes the line -4t once s + t reaches 10. The
          region where profit is positive, running from t = 0 to t = 8 - s,
          is shaded, and it narrows as s increases until it disappears
          entirely at s = 8. A marker tracks Firm 2's best response, which
          sits at the peak of the parabola while one exists and jumps to
          t = 0 once s reaches 8.
        </p>
      </static>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <p>
    <alert>Notice what happens when you compare <m>s \lt 8</m> to
    <m>s \geq 8</m>:</alert>
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <p>
        When <m>s \lt 8</m>: the shaded region is there. Firm 2 earns a
        positive profit for any <m>t</m> with <m>0 \lt t \lt 8 - s</m>.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        When <m>s \geq 8</m>: the shaded region is gone. There is no
        production level left that earns Firm 2 a profit.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The book gives a brief explanation of why this happens, which we'll
    examine in a moment. For now, see if you can use the observation above to
    determine Firm 2's best response in the following scenarios.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-best-response-s9">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Firm 1 produces <m>s = 9</m>. What is Firm 2's best response?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p><m>t = 0</m>. Since <m>9 \geq 8</m>, Firm 2's profit on each unit
          is <m>16 - 18 - 2t = -2 - 2t</m>, negative for every <m>t</m>. Every
          unit loses money, so producing nothing and earning <m>0</m> is the
          best available outcome.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. Firm 2 isn't producing nothing as a strategic move, it's
            producing nothing because every alternative loses money.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p><m>t = 4 - \tfrac{1}{2}(9) = -0.5</m>, from the best-response
          formula.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            This applies the <m>s \lt 8</m> formula outside the range where
            it's valid, and negative production is exactly what the second
            model rules out. It is, though, a good way to see why the second
            case exists: when the parabola's peak lands at a negative
            <m>t</m>, the best allowed choice is the nearest point of the
            domain, <m>t = 0</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p><m>t = 4</m>, the largest value the best-response function
          takes.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            <m>t = 4</m> is <m>b(0)</m>, Firm 2's best response when Firm 1
            produces <em>nothing</em>. That's the opposite end of the range
            from <m>s = 9</m>. Check which case <m>s = 9</m> falls into before
            picking a value of <m>t</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="check-best-response-s7">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Now Firm 1 produces <m>s = 7</m>. What is Firm 2's best response?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p><m>t = 0.5</m>, since <m>7 \lt 8</m> puts us in the first case,
          where <m>b(s) = 4 - \tfrac{1}{2}s = 4 - 3.5</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. The profitable window here is narrow, running only from
            <m>t = 0</m> to <m>t = 8 - 7 = 1</m>, but it isn't empty, and
            Firm 2's best response sits right in the middle of it. Set the
            slider to <m>7</m> and compare.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p><m>t = 0</m>, since <m>s = 7</m> is close enough to <m>8</m> that
          the price is already at Firm 2's cost.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Close isn't the same as reaching it. At <m>s = 7</m> with Firm 2
            producing nothing, the price is <m>20 - 14 = 6</m>, still above
            the cost of <m>4</m>. There's a small profit to be had, and Firm
            2 takes it. The corner solution only kicks in at <m>s = 8</m>
            exactly.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p><m>t = 1</m>, the right-hand edge of the profitable region.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            <m>t = 8 - s = 1</m> is where profit falls back <em>to</em> zero,
            not where it's largest. Producing there earns Firm 2 nothing at
            all. The maximum is at the peak of the parabola, halfway between
            the two places profit equals zero.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    Now let's justify the two cases mathematically, since the book explains
    this only briefly. Look at <m>\pi_2</m> for <m>0 \leq t \lt 10 - s</m>,
    and factor the <m>t</m> out of it instead of multiplying everything
    through:
  </p>

  <me>
    \pi_2(s,t) \;=\;
    \underbrace{\bigl[20 - 2(s+t)\bigr]}_{\text{price } p} t
    \;-\; \underbrace{4}_{\substack{\text{unit} \\ \text{cost}}} t
    \;=\;
    \underbrace{\bigl[\,20 - 2(s+t) - 4\,\bigr]}_{\text{profit on each unit sold}}
    \cdot \underbrace{t}_{\substack{\text{units} \\ \text{sold}}}.
  </me>

  <p>
    Written this way, Firm 2's profit is just <em>profit per unit</em> times
    <em>number of units</em>. Now suppose <m>s \geq 8</m>. Then
  </p>

  <me>
    \underbrace{20 - 2(s+t) - 4}_{\text{profit on each unit}}
      \;=\; 16 - 2s - 2t
      \;\leq\; 16 - 16 - 2t
      \;=\; -2t \;\leq\; 0.
  </me>

  <p>
    So Firm 2 loses money on every unit it makes, and producing <em>more</em>
    only pushes the price further below cost, making the per-unit loss worse.
    The best Firm 2 can do is produce nothing and break even at <m>0</m>.
  </p>

  <p>
    <alert>In contrast, when <m>s \lt 8</m></alert>, the price stays above
    <m>4</m> and Firm 2 does have room to profit. Multiplying out the top
    piece and substituting the fixed <m>s</m> gives the function the book
    graphs in Figure 1.11:
  </p>

  <me>
    \pi_2(s,t) =
    \begin{cases}
      (16 - 2s)t - 2t^2 \amp \text{if } 0 \leq t \lt 10 - s, \\[1ex]
      -4t \amp \text{if } t \geq 10 - s.
    \end{cases}
  </me>

  <p>
    That top piece is exactly the <m>\pi_2</m> from the first model, and it's
    an upside-down parabola, so the derivative test applies to it just as it
    did there. The maximum sits at <m>t = 4 - \tfrac{1}{2}s</m>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Putting the two cases together gives Firm 2's best-response function:
  </p>

  <me>
    b(s) =
    \begin{cases}
      \overbrace{4 - \tfrac{1}{2}s}^{\substack{\text{the peak of the parabola: the same best} \\ \text{response as in the first model}}}
        \amp \text{if } 0 \leq s \lt 8, \\[5ex]
      \underbrace{0}_{\substack{\text{no profitable production level exists, since every } t \gt 0 \\ \text{loses money, so Firm 2 produces nothing and breaks even}}}
        \amp \text{if } s \geq 8.
    \end{cases}
  </me>

  <p>
    That second case is a <alert>corner solution</alert>: the best choice sits
    at the edge of the domain rather than where a derivative vanishes. It's
    the first one in this chapter.
  </p>

  <p>
    Now that we know Firm 2's best-response function <m>b(s)</m>, we proceed
    as before by maximizing Firm 1's payoff function <m>\pi_1(s, b(s))</m>.
    This time that means dealing with piecewise functions, since <m>\pi_1</m>
    and <m>b(s)</m> are <em>both</em> piecewise. Before doing any
    computation, though, we can narrow down where the maximum has to live.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-which-case-holds-s-star">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Without computing anything, can you tell which case the optimal
        <m>s^*</m> falls into: <m>0 \leq s \lt 8</m>, or <m>s \geq 8</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p><m>0 \leq s^* \lt 8</m>. If Firm 1 produced <m>s \geq 8</m> it
          would drive the price down to its <em>own</em> cost of <m>4</m> or
          below, so it would earn nothing or lose money. Since Firm 1 can earn
          a positive profit with a smaller <m>s</m>, the maximum can't be out
          there.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. The threshold <m>s = 8</m> was defined by the price
            hitting <m>4</m>, and both firms have that same unit cost, so what
            kills Firm 2's profit at <m>s \geq 8</m> kills Firm 1's too. This
            is why the book says it won't bother computing
            <m>\pi_1(s, b(s))</m> for <m>s \geq 8</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p><m>s^* \geq 8</m>, since that drives Firm 2 out of the market
          entirely and leaves Firm 1 as the only producer.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            It's true that Firm 2 produces nothing when <m>s \geq 8</m>, but
            look at what it costs Firm 1 to get there. Being the only seller
            is worthless if the price you had to create is at or below your
            own cost of <m>4</m>. Firm 1 wants profit, not market share.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p><m>s^* \geq 8</m>, because profit is price times quantity, so
          producing more units always earns more.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Profit is <em>margin</em> times quantity, not price times
            quantity, and the margin shrinks as production rises. Past
            <m>s = 8</m> the margin is zero or negative, so extra units make
            things worse rather than better.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>There's no way to tell in advance. Both pieces have to be
          maximized and the two results compared.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            That's always a safe fallback, but here one piece can be ruled out
            by an argument rather than a computation: at <m>s \geq 8</m> the
            price is at or below Firm 1's own cost of <m>4</m>, so its profit
            there can't be positive.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    So we only need to work on <m>0 \leq s \lt 8</m>. There's still one thing
    to check before writing down a formula: <m>\pi_1</m> is itself piecewise,
    split at <m>s + t = 10</m>, so we need to know which piece we land on once
    <m>t</m> is replaced by <m>b(s)</m>. For <m>0 \leq s \lt 8</m>,
  </p>

  <me>
    s + b(s) \;=\; s + 4 - \tfrac{1}{2}s \;=\; 4 + \tfrac{1}{2}s
      \;\lt\; 4 + \tfrac{1}{2}(8) \;=\; 8 \;\lt\; 10.
  </me>

  <exercise xml:id="check-which-piece-of-pi1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        What does the computation above tell us?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>Total production <m>s + b(s)</m> never reaches <m>10</m> on this
          range, so <m>\pi_1</m> always stays on its first piece. We can use
          <m>\bigl[20 - 2(s+t)\bigr]s - 4s</m> and get
          <m>\pi_1(s, b(s)) = 8s - s^2</m>, exactly as in the first model.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct, and this is the payoff for pinning down Firm 2's best
            response first. Once <m>t = b(s)</m>, total production is capped
            at <m>8</m>, so the second piece of <m>\pi_1</m> is unreachable
            and the piecewise complication never actually bites.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>The price stays positive, but we still have to maximize the
          second piece of <m>\pi_1</m> separately and compare.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            The second piece applies only when <m>s + t \geq 10</m>, and the
            computation shows <m>s + b(s) \lt 8</m> for every <m>s</m> in this
            range. There's no <m>s</m> left for that piece to describe, so
            there's nothing to compare against.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>Firm 2 always produces less than Firm 1.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            That happens to be false at small <m>s</m> (at <m>s = 0</m>, Firm
            2 produces <m>4</m>), and in any case it isn't what the
            computation is about. The quantity being bounded is the
            <em>total</em> <m>s + b(s)</m>, which is what decides which piece
            of <m>\pi_1</m> is in force.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>It tells us directly that <m>s^* = 4</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not yet. This only establishes <em>which formula</em> to maximize.
            Finding <m>s^*</m> still takes a derivative, which is the next
            step.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    From here the work is identical to Part 1: maximizing
    <m>\pi_1(s, b(s)) = 8s - s^2</m> on <m>0 \leq s \lt 8</m> gives
    <m>s^* = 4</m>, and Firm 2 answers with
    <m>t^* = b(4) = 4 - \tfrac{1}{2}(4) = 2</m>. The price is
    <m>p^* = 20 - 2(4+2) = 8</m>, and the profits are <m>16</m> for Firm 1 and
    <m>8</m> for Firm 2.
  </p>

  <p>
    Notice that the more realistic model produces the exact same answer as the
    first one! Here's a quick recap of the two places the restrictions forced
    us to do extra work:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <p>
        <alert>Finding <m>b(s)</m>.</alert> For <m>s \geq 8</m>, Firm 1 has
        already pushed the price down to <m>4</m> or below, so every unit
        Firm 2 makes loses money and its best response is <m>b(s) = 0</m>.
        For <m>s \lt 8</m> there's still a profitable window, and on that
        window <m>\pi_2</m> is the same function as in the first model, and we find the same answer
        <m>b(s) = 4 - \tfrac{1}{2}s</m>.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <alert>Maximizing <m>\pi_1(s, b(s))</m>.</alert> We ruled out
        <m>s \geq 8</m> for the same reason as above: the price is at or below
        Firm 1's own cost of <m>4</m>, so producing there loses money too. On
        <m>0 \leq s \lt 8</m> we found <m>s + b(s) = 4 + \tfrac{1}{2}s \lt
        10</m>, which keeps <m>\pi_1</m> on its first piece, the same formula
        as in the first model. So the maximization is the one we already did,
        and it gives the same <m>s^*</m>, <m>t^*</m>, price, and profits.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-1-11">
    <title>Section 1.11, Stackelberg's Model of Duopoly 2</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Skim Section 1.11.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      This section redoes both models in a more general setting: the price
      rule becomes <m>p = \alpha - \beta(s+t)</m> and the unit cost becomes
      <m>c</m>. The procedure is exactly the one you just used, so there's no
      need to read every line carefully. What you should come away able to do
      is adapt the method from Section 1.10 when the price and cost are
      something other than <m>20 - 2(s+t)</m> and <m>4</m>.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    One observation worth pulling out of that section: the general answer is
    <m>s^* = \dfrac{\alpha - c}{2\beta}</m> and
    <m>t^* = \dfrac{\alpha - c}{4\beta}</m>, with profits
    <m>\dfrac{(\alpha-c)^2}{8\beta}</m> and
    <m>\dfrac{(\alpha-c)^2}{16\beta}</m>. Firm 1 produces twice as much and
    earns twice as much no matter what <m>\alpha</m>, <m>\beta</m>, and
    <m>c</m> are, so the 2-to-1 split we found wasn't an accident of our
    particular numbers. It's what moving first is worth in any model of this
    shape.
  </p>
<interactive xml:id="question-form-1-6" iframe="question-form.html?section=1.6" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>
</section>
